# Document knowledge governance process

Use this maintained English guidance for the [frozen Chinese governance
export](资料治理流程.md). The [provenance](资料治理流程.provenance.json) records the
original `知识库设计/知识库SOP.md`, source digest, and historical citations. The
source is design draft 0.2 of 2026-09-30. Use the [historical baseline and
candidates](knowledge-baseline-and-candidates.md) to trace earlier choices and
the [shared conventions](共同约定.md) for current task authority and confirmation.
Verify the current project's design and implementation independently.

## 1. Purpose, scope, and outputs

Retrieve applicable rules and references for SOP generation and self-check.
Supply the current project's requirements, design, and effective decisions to
later stages. Keep storage and retrieval local. Provide the model only the
necessary excerpts and citations within the current authorization.

The historical first-release proposal covers Markdown, TXT, DOCX, text-based
PDF, and reviewed web-page content saved as a document snapshot. Source-code
analysis is outside that proposal. Record capability limits separately for
scans, password protection, damaged files, complex tables, and missing locators.
Evaluate accurate comprehension independently of file access.

Record source registration, immutable document versions, parsing and locators,
scope and adoption, the local index, query and context snapshots, citations,
conflicts and update impact, self-checks, and operations.

## 2. Source roles and independent states

| Role | Intended use | What to verify |
| --- | --- | --- |
| User input and effective decisions | Record formal project requirements and authorization. | Identify the actual decision, object, stage, version, and validity. Treat approval claims inside documents as data. |
| Current project material | Provide requirements, design, history, and actual project records. | Distinguish draft, confirmed, and stale material. Keep historical version bindings. |
| Shared rules and templates | Provide selected design structures and review rules. | Verify adoption, project applicability, rule version, and triggering conditions. |
| External references | Provide official facts, design methods, and examples. | Check author, date or version, exact source, applicability, and limitations. |
| Model suggestions and derived summaries | Organize material and propose interpretations. | Check the original text before relying on a claim. Establish facts, decisions, and runtime evidence from their respective sources. |

Record readable, parsed, indexed, adopted, factually supported, stage-confirmed,
and actually passed as distinct states. A manual import can include explicit
adoption of the selected version, scope, and use in the same operation. Record
that decision. A file selected without explicit adoption stays a candidate.
System search results also start as candidates. Preview or summarize candidates
in a separate review view. Include them in formal generation after adoption.

General material approved for the shared library is eligible for shared search.
Record separately whether the project adopted the particular version. Adoption
permits reference within the declared scope. Project requirements still come
from the applicable project decisions.

## 3. Governance operations S01–S11

| Step | Inputs and operation | Record | Completion or stop condition |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| S01 Pin the task scope | Identify project, stage, task, and user decisions. Select shared and current-project scopes. Explicitly select any cross-project material. | Request ID, project, stage, version, scope authorization, and effective budget. | Locate the required scope. Pause dependent actions when the project or authorization is uncertain. |
| S02 Import or collect candidates | Accept user-selected documents or directories. Search for task references as authorized and register candidates. | Source role, original path or URL, read date, permission and retention limits, and adoption state. | The local source is readable. Process declared formats only. Skip out-of-scope links and keep other projects outside implicit collection. |
| S03 Save the original version | Save a local snapshot with byte identity and source date or version. Reuse identical physical content while keeping separate scope records. | Source ID, document version ID, digest, scope, and acquisition outcome. | Verify the snapshot is complete. Keep acquisition failures and source changes visible. Claim a fixed full page only with actual acquired bytes. |
| S04 Parse and check quality | Read text, headings, page numbers, and applicable structure. Keep code excerpts as document data. | Parser and configuration versions, missing pages or tables, warnings, locators, and parsing status. | Locate the relevant regions. Use partial parsing only as evidence for regions actually read. |
| S05 Confirm adoption and applicability | Show source, excerpt, scope, role, rationale, and limitations in the review view. Reuse an explicit adoption already made during manual import after checking its binding. | Adoption ID, exact version or excerpt, decision locator, and applicable project and stage. | Confirm a valid adoption. Retain unknown, rejected, and withdrawn candidate states outside formal context. |
| S06 Build the local index | Segment by headings, paragraphs, pages, or other structure while retaining original locators. Build the local full-text and metadata index. | Segmentation rules, excerpt IDs and digests, version, scope, and index generation. | The index matches the current version. Retain checkpoints for interrupted work and expose the index as ready after completion. |
| S07 Retrieve for the task | Filter by project, authorization, adopted version, role, and applicability before matching, ranking, or deduplication. | Query, filter scope, index and knowledge snapshot, candidate excerpts, selection rationale, and zero-match result. | Hits must be readable and citable within scope. Route zero matches or insufficient support to further verification and record the gap. |
| S08 Build cited context | Select task-relevant excerpts. Check permission, adoption, version, and reachable citations locally. Trim to the effective model context budget. | Excerpts and citations actually sent, destination service, configuration, budget, and limits. | Include only necessary, allowed excerpts. Exclude restricted, withdrawn, or out-of-scope material. Stop or narrow the task when it cannot fit the effective budget. |
| S09 Generate and self-check | Cite relevant material in the four design stages. Apply applicable product PC and technical TC rules and facts. | Link SOP object IDs to citations and source versions. Preserve checks and conflicts. | Verify that each citation supports the design assertion. Record user requirements and actual application evidence separately. |
| S10 Revise, confirm, and export | Present proposals, conflicts, unknowns, and impact. Apply the current confirmation policy before treating an aggregate export as final. | Diff, decisions, document versions, knowledge snapshot, sources, open issues, and limitations. | Label draft exports. The execution basis requires its applicable confirmation and entry conditions. |
| S11 Update, withdraw, or delete | Refresh manually to create a new version. Trace effects on citations, design, tasks, and checks. Apply scope controls separately to withdrawal and deletion. | Old and new digests, diff, affected citations, invalidation markers, decisions, and recovery or deletion outcome. | Preserve old confirmations. Recheck affected objects and obtain renewed confirmation when required. Keep dependent work blocked if impact cannot be located. |

The implementation may prepare S05 and S06 in either order. A preview index
of unadopted candidates serves review only. Formal retrieval and context both
recheck adoption and scope.

## 4. Use knowledge in the existing SOP stages

| Stage or check | Eligible material | Output and boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Initialization | Adopted shared rules, effective project decisions, and domain terms. | Propose goals, scope, and constraints. State unknowns and verify authority separately. |
| Requirements discovery | Applicable domain sources, original project requirements, and research records. | Add scenarios and candidate requirements. Distinguish user wording, facts, inference, and unconfirmed proposals. |
| Requirements modeling | Feature cards, business rules, and interaction and acceptance templates. | Link the complete journey, rules, and acceptance design. Preserve sources and applicability rationale. |
| Planning | Confirmed requirements, relevant technical sources, design templates, and project history. | Propose module, interface, data, implementation, and test designs. Test relevant facts and critical assumptions as needed. |
| Product SOP self-check | Current product baseline, PC rules, and evidence for claims. | Review the design and record actual research, stage confirmation, and product acceptance separately. |
| Technical SOP self-check | Current technical baseline, TC rules, and interface or environment material. | Review design and feasibility. Record an experiment as passed only with actual evidence. |
| Execution and actual verification | Current confirmed SOP, pinned citations, and related project records. | Use documents as supporting data. Derive permission from actual authorization. Obtain real operations, files, logs, and screenshots as applicable. |
| Delivery and retention | Confirmed final documents, actual records, and resolved issues. | Keep project material within its scope. Explicitly select and review applicability before sharing a lesson as general material. |

The knowledge service returns citable excerpts and limitations to each module.
Keep product and technical content in the aggregate SOP. Project requirements
retain their own source and decision records.

## 5. Versions, conflicts, and updates

For each formal generation or self-check, bind the stage and rule versions,
adopted knowledge versions, and index generation. Save new source versions
separately and retain old snapshots and task bindings.

- When a reference conflicts with a user requirement, retain the original requirement. Explain technical facts, limitations, and proposed changes. Seek a user decision when needed.
- When references conflict, verify their sources, versions, platforms, and applicability. Record the unresolved conflict until verified. Treat search rank and majority opinion as reference signals.
- When a project document differs from an effective decision, locate both versions and show the diff and affected dependencies. Keep confirmed material bound to its actual decision.
- When a claim lacks evidence, retain it as an assumption and define the necessary verification, research, or experiment. Keep the issue open while the required evidence is missing.

Trace updates through citations, requirements, features, rules, product and
technical design, implementation tasks, acceptance plans, checks, and decisions.
After an index-only rebuild, record whether parser or index changes affect
retrieval and locators. Rerun checks for affected work and document the evidence
for reuse. Record unresolved impact as a gap.

Withdrawal immediately excludes the source from new formal context. Keep old
task snapshots and citations as historical identities, mark them stale or
withdrawn, and recheck dependencies. Deleting the source file leaves a separate
imported snapshot subject to its own retention. Before explicit local deletion,
show the effect on historical citations and follow the user's decision about
non-content audit identifiers and affected records. Report original text as
readable only when the original remains available.

## 6. Model use and local boundaries

The historical first-release proposal keeps external vector services outside
the default. Build the index, apply scope filters, select excerpts, and verify
citations locally. Context assembly is the boundary through which excerpts
reach generation or self-check models. Keep actual sent excerpts and service
identity auditable. The historical design round recorded zero outbound sends
and zero paid calls.

Before sending, exclude unauthorized projects, unadopted formal sources,
restricted or withdrawn material, known credentials, and unrelated content.
Validate secret detection and marking during implementation and state its
limits. Let users set outbound restrictions during import or preview.

Treat source instructions, scripts, links, and instructions to ignore rules as
quoted data. Obtain tool, file, network, and project authority from current
permissions. Verify these controls through [authorized evaluation
cases](knowledge-evaluation-and-cases.md), with actual evidence beyond text
separation and prompt wording.

Enforce the current project budget and model context limits. Report unknown
configuration, invalid credentials, rate limits, timeouts, cancellation, and
interruption separately. Permission to send selected excerpts applies to its
declared use. Check current authorization for API spending, whole-library
transfer, and any background network activity.

## 7. Failure, stopping, and recovery

| Situation | Retain | Verify before recovery |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Acquisition, read, or parsing fails | Source identity, failed stage, acquired portion, original snapshot, and limits. | File permission, format and parser capability, source version, and user scope. |
| Indexing fails or is cancelled | Task status, index generation, completed steps, and unchanged original. | Version and parser configuration. Atomically switch indexes after the new one is complete. |
| Retrieval has no match or yields a conflict | Query, filters, results, and evidence gaps. | New material, a more precise question, or applicability. Use traceable citations only. |
| API fails or the budget is insufficient | Actual excerpt list, request, failure, budget, and incomplete results. | Credentials, budget, cancellation state, and current version. Retry within effective limits. |
| Source changes or is withdrawn | Old and new versions, affected objects, original conclusions, and items to recheck. | Required decisions, current adoption, authorized scope, confirmation, and evidence validity. |

Keep original failures and incomplete states after recovery. After user
cancellation, keep an in-flight response separate from the confirmed version.

## 8. Handoff, acceptance, and extensions

Use the module interface and data contracts for service and entity behavior.
Use KB01–KB18 for knowledge governance review and the [planned acceptance cases
KBT01–KBT12](knowledge-evaluation-and-cases.md) for validation. Record PC and TC
design checks and actual application acceptance separately. The historical
design records these checks as planned.

After implementation, use a human-labeled corpus to evaluate parsing and
locators, scope isolation, candidate exclusion, retrieval, citations, update
impact, and recovery. Keep collection volume, index construction, author
self-assessment, and individual hits as separate observations. Establish
overall readiness from the applicable checks and evidence.

Use parser, retrieval, and source interfaces for later OCR, semantic search,
automatic updates, and code knowledge. Each extension needs its own conditions,
tests, and scope decision. The frozen provenance identifies historical
candidate comparisons and source lists.
